   886	#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are per context, and completion is conservative
   887	
   888	A name extracted from code is an **obligation** of every activation that
   889	can execute that code, and an obligation is discharged only by a binding
   890	that activation would actually see under `resolve-var`'s precedence
   891	(`engine.cljc:46-65`). Resolution is activation-specific: the reference
   892	resolver returns the primitive for `x` under an empty environment and the
   893	environment's binding under one that binds `x`. So a binding discharges
   894	an obligation only in one of these contexts:
   895	
   896	| Obligation from | Discharged by |
   897	|---|---|
   898	| a `:variable` inside a `:lambda` body naming one of that lambda's params, or an enclosing lambda's params on the structural path | the parameter binding, **only when call analysis establishes that the binding exists** (below); otherwise the name is an ordinary obligation of the closure's context |
   899	| code the active control points into | the active frame's environment, or the store slice by key |
   900	| a closure's body (the segment its `:entry` points into) | that closure's captured `:env`, or the store slice |
   901	| code a K frame, a parked record, or a wait entry resumes into | that frame's or record's own `:env`, or the store slice |
   902	| code reachable only by address, with no activation associated | nothing: the obligation is retained |
   903	
   904	**A parameter is bound only when an argument was supplied for it.** The
   905	reference machine binds parameters with `(zipmap params args)` and checks
   906	no arity (`semantic.cljc:199-205`; the walker does the same,
   907	`ast_walker.cljc:190-191`), so an under-arity call leaves the omitted
   908	parameters unbound and a `:variable` naming one resolves through the
   909	captured environment, the store, the primitives, and the modules
   910	(`engine.cljc:46-65`). A parameter obligation at index `i` is therefore
   911	discharged for a lambda only when **every** call site of that lambda is
   912	known and every one supplies at least `i + 1` arguments; call sites are
   913	known only when the closure does not escape the analyzed code, that is,
   914	when every `:application` whose operator can evaluate to it is in the
   915	analyzed trees and the closure value does not appear in any carried
   916	environment, store slice, stack, or parked record. Otherwise the name
   917	stays an obligation of the closure's context: it is discharged by the
   918	captured `:env` or the store slice, and if neither binds it, it is
   919	retained as a primitive or module requirement. An unknown call context
   920	retains or yields `:incomplete`; it never discharges. This design does
   921	not introduce an exact-arity rule, which would be an execution-contract
   922	amendment outside its scope.
   923	
   924	**An unrelated carried binding never discharges an obligation.** A saved
   925	continuation that binds `x` says nothing about the active activation's
   926	`x`, which may resolve to a primitive with a profile the value needs. Every
   927	retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by
   928	profile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its
   929	profile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`
   930	contributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`
   931	because their profiles say so. Where the analysis cannot associate an
   932	occurrence with a context, or a retained name has no profile at the
   933	emitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every
   934	possible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never
   935	makes a `:complete` result wrong.
   936	
   937	This rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its
   938	sentence that a name "is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the
   939	value carries" is the unsound test, and this document states the
   940	per-context rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same
   941	(§10.8).
   942	
   943	#### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses
   944	
   945	The unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an
   946	address. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured
   947	environments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its
   948	own environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so carries different
   949	obligations. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the
   950	set of names bound in the activation's environment (captured `:env` keys
   951	plus parameters established under §7.7.2's call analysis), and nothing
   952	else. Names come from finite code and finite carried values, so the set
   953	of contexts is finite and the iteration terminates.
   954	
   955	- **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected
   956	  once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is
   957	  analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.
   958	- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the
   959	  carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3); a missing one is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`
   960	  naming the id.
   961	- **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the
   962	  K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait
   963	  contribute work items and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies; a
   964	  closure contributes `[its segment address, its captured-env context]`.
   965	- **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets
   966	  `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.
   967	
   968	**Convergence** is reached when one full pass adds nothing to any of:
   969	the work-item set, the obligation set (name × context), the discovered
   970	value set (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), the store-key
   971	requirement, the FFI-op requirement, the effect set after normalization
   972	(§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new
   973	context, a new profile fact, or a new footprint continues the iteration
   974	even when no new address appeared. If an implementation cannot compute
   975	the bound-name set for some context, it reports `:incomplete` rather than
   976	terminating on address stability.
   977	
   978	Discovery is `:complete` only when every reachable work item was
   979	analyzed, every obligation was discharged in its own context or retained
   980	as a profiled requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module
   981	footprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be
   982	fetched and `:incomplete` otherwise. Recasting the walk as Datalog changes
   983	where the facts come from, not the conservatism of the closure.
   984	
   985	UCF §7.6.1 has the same defect in its own terms: "repeats until nothing
   986	new is reachable" is stated over segments and values, and its walk of "a
   987	newly fetched segment" is per address. It needs this same amendment
   988	(§10.8).
   989	
   990	Because the queries are over data, they answer for a tree that has never
   991	run, which is the property §7.6.1 names.
