   995	## §8 The ledger and refs
   996	
   997	### 8.1 Two notations, one relation
   998	
   999	The ledger is one logical relation with rows `[t op address ref]`, ruling
  1000	6's shape: `t` is the ledger's clock, `op` is drawn from an open
  1001	vocabulary, `address` is a content address, and `ref` is the row's other
  1002	argument, a name entity or a second address. This document writes ledger
  1003	facts in that notation when it speaks of the relation.
  1004	
  1005	The **persistent representation** of a row is datoms, and a datom is bound
  1006	by `datom.md`'s contract: `e`, `t`, and `m` are stream-local, `e` is a
  1007	non-negative integer, `m` is an integer, and `v` is the only slot that may
  1008	hold a foreign reference (`datom.md:140-180`). The transactor enforces
  1009	this: `local-datom?` (`src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:42-58`) rejects a
  1010	non-integer `e` or `m`, and `pad-datom`
  1011	(`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:74-90`) rejects a caller-supplied `t`
  1012	and stamps its own. Therefore a content address never appears in `e`, an
  1013	op keyword never appears in `m`, and `t` is never chosen by the writer.
  1014	The mapping between notations is:
  1015	
  1016	| Logical slot | Persistent carrier |
  1017	|---|---|
  1018	| `t` | the datom `t` the transactor stamped |
  1019	| `op` | for naming rows: the reserved validity marker in `m` (`:db/assert` 1, `:db/retract` 0); for every other op: the `:yin.ledger/op` value of a **ledger event entity** (§8.2) |
  1020	| `address` | a `v` slot |
  1080	a metadata entity's `:db/op` for Datomic-visible validity is the
  1081	prerequisite `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names and is outside this
  1082	design.
  1083	
  1084	The vocabulary is open: a composition that needs a new op declares a new
  1085	keyword and a record shape. A reader dispatches on the ops it understands
  1086	(`[?ev :yin.ledger/op :derive]`) and does not see the others, which is a
  1087	property of the query it writes, not of the fold.
  1088	
  1089	### 8.3 Rows of the one relation
  1090	
  1091	| Logical row | Persistent form |
  1092	|---|---|
  1093	| `[t :assert tree-addr name]` — a name refers to a tree | `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]` |
  1094	| `[t :retract tree-addr name]` — the name no longer does | `[:db/retract name :yin/code tree-addr]`, followed by the new assertion |
  1095	| `[t :derive seg-addr tree-addr]` — a segment was lowered from a tree | a `:derive` event entity (§8.2); direction: `input` is the tree, `output` is the segment |
  1096	| `[t :assert seg-addr k-occurrence]` — a segment is the address a continuation names | `[:db/add occ :yin.k/segment seg-addr]` on the local occurrence entity |
  1097	| `[t :expand tree-out tree-in]` — a tree is the expansion of another | an `:expand` event entity (§8.4) |
  1098	| `[t :assert lambda-addr name]` under `:yin.macro/definition` | not a ledger row at all: macro-ness is an occurrence-bound declaration beside the tree (§8.5); the ledger records only the expander's harvest result, `[:db/add name :yin.macro/definition lambda-addr]`, for queries |
  1099	| `[t :publish ucf-addr occurrence]` — a checkpoint occurrence was published | a `:publish` event entity; custody stays `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged (UCF §7.7.2), keyed by the same local occurrence entity |
  1100	| `[t :assert tree-addr manifest]` — a module manifest pins a tree | `[:db/add manifest :yin.module/exports tree-addr]` |
  1101	
  1102	`:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name as a query alias for
  1103	"the `:yin.ledger/input` of a `:derive` event whose output is this
  1104	address" and is no longer an attribute of the segment (`v2.cljc:327`,
  1105	`linearize.cljc:212`).
  1106	
  1107	### 8.4 Expansion events keep their identity
  1108	
  1109	`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 records one event per attempt, successful or not,
  1110	including repeated identical expansions, and links the event to its output
  1111	(`yin.vm.macro.md:666-709`). This design keeps that contract: the
  1112	`:expand` event entity **is** that event, one per attempt, and the address
  1113	rows are attributes of it, not a replacement for it:
  1114	
  1115	```clojure
  1116	[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op         :expand]
  1117	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input      tree-in-addr]        ; the tree containing the call
  1118	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-root  tree-in-addr]        ; the call occurrence, as values:
  1119	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-path  path]                ;   root address and structural path
  1120	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/origin     [:source medium batch j]]   ; INITIAL expansion only: a value
  1121	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent-event parent-ev]         ; NESTED expansion only: a declared ref, log-local
  1122	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent     parent-record-addr]  ; NESTED expansion only: the parent's record address, portable
  1123	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output     tree-out-addr]       ; on success
  1124	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/macro      lambda-addr]         ; the macro's content
  1125	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/attempt    n]                   ; per-expander attempt counter
  1126	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record     record-addr]         ; the addressed record of §8.2
  1127	 [:db/add ev :yin/source-batch      batch-id]            ; unchanged from §4.1
  1128	 [:db/add ev :yin/error             {...}]               ; on failure, unchanged
  1129	 [:db/add ev :yin/timestamp         ctx-t]]              ; unchanged
  1130	```
  1131	
  1132	The split between local references and portable values follows
  1133	`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 exactly (`yin.vm.macro.md:692-708`), and it is
  1134	enforced by how the transactor resolves ids: `apply-tempid-map` replaces a
  1135	tempid in `v` **only when the whole value is a tempid on a declared
  1136	reference attribute** (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:168-185`); a
  1137	tempid nested inside a vector is left as it is. Therefore:
  1138	
  1139	- an **initial** expansion's call sits in admitted syntax, so it carries
  1140	  `:yin.ledger/origin`, a value `[:source medium batch j]` including the
  1141	  batch member index: the successor of `:yin/source-call` qualified by
  1142	  `:yin/source-batch`, which no transactor resolves, as today;
  1143	- a **nested** expansion's call sits in the output of the enclosing
  1144	  expansion, so it carries **two** links to the parent: `:yin.ledger/
  1145	  parent-event`, a declared reference attribute whose whole value is the
  1146	  parent event's id, the successor of `:yin/source-node` and resolved by
  1147	  the transactor within the log medium exactly as `:yin/source-node` is
  1148	  today; and `:yin.ledger/parent`, the parent's **record address**, which
  1149	  is a value and travels. The parent is complete before the child begins,
  1150	  because a nested expansion's input is the enclosing expansion's output
  1151	  (`yin.vm.macro.md:698-701`), so the parent's record address is known
  1152	  when the child's record is minted; no staging after allocation is
  1153	  needed, and the record (§8.2) never contains a log-local id, so it is
  1154	  hashed once, when built.
  1155	
  1156	A successful event carries exactly one of `:yin.ledger/origin` and the
  1157	parent pair; an admission failure carries neither. The chain `source →
  1158	event → output → event → output` of §4.1 is walked locally through
  1159	`:yin.ledger/parent-event` and portably through `:yin.ledger/parent`, and
  1160	two attempts that produced identical output trees are two events with two
  1161	ids and two record addresses (the records differ in `:yin.ledger/attempt`
  1162	and origin or parent), so a child names its parent without ambiguity in
  1163	either walk. `:yin/expansion-root` and `:yin/macro` of §4.1 are carried by
  1164	`:yin.ledger/output` and `:yin.ledger/macro` as addresses; the other event
  1165	attributes are unchanged. Because the *content* values are addresses, the
  1166	cross-media identity problem of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 does not arise for
  1167	content: the same tree has the same address on `program-in`,
  1168	`program-out`, and the log. The local event id remains log-local, as
  1169	`:yin/source-node` is today; the record address is what leaves the log.
